17	/*
18	 * The user window under the Cortex-M0+ MPU.
19	 *
20	 * One process is resident at a time and every process image occupies the
21	 * same 144 KB at USER_DATA_START (machparam.h, conf/RP2040.ld), so the
22	 * protection map is static: the kernel programs it once at startup and no
23	 * context switch touches it. Unprivileged code, which locore0.S enters by
24	 * setting CONTROL.nPRIV before the first user instruction, may reach three
25	 * regions and nothing else:
26	 *
27	 *   region  base         size    unprivileged  holds
28	 *   0       0x00000000   16 KB   r-x           the boot ROM, whose float
29	 *                                              routines libc calls through
30	 *                                              lib/libc/arm/gen/rom_float_resolver.S
31	 *   1       0x20000000   128 KB  rwx           the user window, low part
32	 *   2       0x20020000   16 KB   rwx           the user window, high part
33	 *
34	 * 144 KB is not a power of two and PMSAv6 regions are (datasheet table
35	 * 117, SIZE gives 2^(SIZE+1) bytes), so the window takes two regions, each
36	 * at a base aligned to its own size. Text runs from the window because
37	 * a.out images are loaded into SRAM, so XN stays clear on both. The kernel
38	 * runs privileged and MPU_CTRL.PRIVDEFENA keeps the default memory map as
39	 * its background region, so kernel text in XIP flash, kernel data above
40	 * the window, the peripherals and the PPB stay reachable to the kernel and
41	 * closed to a process. A user access outside the three regions raises
42	 * HardFault (datasheet 2.4.6.1), which fault.c delivers as SIGSEGV to the
43	 * process on its own stack.
44	 *
45	 * Every write is read back. A region counts as programmed only when RBAR
46	 * and RASR return what was written, and the MPU is enabled only when all
47	 * three do: a map missing a user region would fault the first user
48	 * instruction, so a core or emulator that drops the writes runs with the
49	 * MPU off and says so through mpu_identify and machdep.mpu. The memory
50	 * attribute bits S, C and B stay zero; the RP2040 bus fabric consumes none
51	 * of them (the XIP cache is an address-range cache), and the Cortex-M0+
52	 * reorders nothing.
53	 *
54	 * usr.bin/mputest is the deliberate fault test: it forks children that
55	 * read kernel RAM, kernel flash and SIO and expects each to die of SIGSEGV
56	 * when machdep.mpu.enable reads 1.
57	 */
